The whole proof is Pythagoras: the radius to the point of contact is perpendicular to the tangent, so \(C\), \(T\), \(P\) form a right triangle with the right angle at \(T\) and hypotenuse \(CP\).
Notice that the expression under the root is the same number \(S_{1}\) as in Theorem 1 (6.2.4): \(S_{1} = |CP|^{2} - r^{2}\). So:
\(S_{1} > 0\) (outside): a real tangent of length \(\sqrt{S_{1}}\);
\(S_{1} = 0\) (on the circle): length 0, the point is its own point of contact;
\(S_{1} < 0\) (inside): no real tangent, which matches Theorem 2.
Both tangents from \(P\) have the same length (the two right triangles share \(CP\) and have equal radii).
